  "question": {
    "text": "Take the mean distance of the moon and the sun from the earth to be <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0.4</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>6</mn></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>km</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>150</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>6</mn></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>km</mi></math>, respectively. Their masses are <math><mi>&#160;</mi><mn>8</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>22</mn></mrow></msup></math> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>kg</mi></math>&nbsp;and <math><mn>2</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>30</mn></mrow></msup></math> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>kg</mi></math>, respectively. The radius of the earth is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>6400</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>km</mi></math>. Let <math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#916;</mi><msub><mrow><mi>F</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></math> be the difference in the forces exerted by the moon at the nearest and farthest point on the earth, and <math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#916;</mi><msub><mrow><mi>F</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></math> be the difference in the forces exerted by the sun at the nearest and farthest points on the earth. Then, the number closest to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#916;</mi><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#916;</mi><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac></math> is,"
  },
